Found while reviewing #2165 (the same class, found there first on Snowflake).

`databricks_dqx._literal` escapes a string value for DQX by doubling single quotes only. DQX evaluates each `is_in_list` value with `F.expr`, and **Spark SQL processes backslash escapes inside string literals** (`spark.sql.parser.escapedStringLiterals` defaults to false). So a backslash in an allowed value can escape the closing quote.

**Live-confirmed 2026-09-28** on `samples.tpch.part`, with DataQ's merged `UnityCatalogCheckRunner.run_native_checks`:
- `["x\\' || p_container || \\'"]` became `'x\'' || p_container || \'''`, which is no longer a literal but an expression that references a column.
- `["ends\\"]` left an unterminated literal.
- Result: `PARSE_SYNTAX_ERROR … near '\'`, which **failed the whole job**, so every DQX check in the run errored, including an unrelated control rule (`'SM CASE'`).

**Impact:** a check author (Member+) could make an allowed value evaluate as an arbitrary Spark SQL expression inside the connection's workspace, with the token's permissions. It also lets one bad value take down every DQX check in a suite.

**Fix:**
1. Escape backslashes before quotes.
2. Make the runner notebook fall back to applying each rule on its own when the combined pass fails, so any rule that errors at run time errors only itself.
3. Add tests and re-verify live.
